The @code{sb-fiber} module, loadable by

@example
(require :sb-fiber)
@end example

provides user-space coroutines, called fibers, with their own
control and binding stacks. A fiber runs on one OS thread at a
time, switches to another fiber only when it asks to, and can be
moved between threads. The module is built when SBCL is configured
with @code{--with-sb-fiber}, on x86-64 and arm64 without safepoints and
not on win32; the feature is @code{:sb-fiber}.

@code{sb-fiber:switch-fiber} is the one control transfer. It is the
building block for schedulers, I/O integration and fiber-aware
synchronization in user code; the module itself schedules nothing.

A switch preserves the resumed fiber's control stack, binding
stack, @code{*handler-clusters*}, @code{*restart-clusters*}, catch and
unwind-protect chains, callee-saved registers and, on x86-64, the
SSE control word. A fiber's entry function runs with fresh
@code{*handler-clusters*} and @code{*restart-clusters*}. A condition that
leaves the entry function unhandled is stored on the fiber and
signalled again in the fiber that resumes it.

Fibers do not survive @code{sb-ext:save-lisp-and-die}: a saved core starts
with no fibers, and references to fibers kept across a save are
stale. A suspended fiber's C stack is scanned conservatively by the
collector, on arm64 as well as x86-64.

@example
(require :sb-fiber)
(use-package :sb-fiber)

(with-fiber-thread ()
  (let ((child (make-fiber
                (lambda ()
                  (format t "hello from child~%")
                  (yield-fiber)
                  (format t "back again~%")))))
    (resume-fiber child)
    (format t "main resumed~%")
    (resume-fiber child)))
@end example

prints @code{hello from child}, @code{main resumed} and @code{back again}. A
scheduler layers non-blocking I/O on this: a fiber that would block
on a descriptor registers it with poll, epoll or kqueue and yields,
and the scheduler's poll loop resumes the fiber when the descriptor
is ready.

@node sb fiber threads
@subsection Fibers and threads

A thread takes part in fiber switching once it has a main fiber,
which stands for the thread's own stacks. @code{sb-fiber:with-fiber-thread}
makes one for the extent of its body; @code{sb-fiber:make-main-fiber} and
@code{sb-fiber:with-current-fiber} are the explicit forms for a library
that runs fibers on threads it does not own and keeps a main fiber
registered between uses. Every fiber belongs to one thread, the only
thread that may switch into it, until @code{sb-fiber:fiber-migrate} hands
it to another.

@anchor{Macro sb-fiber with-fiber-thread}
@ffindex @sortas{with-fiber-thread sb-fiber} with-fiber-thread [sb-fiber]
@deffn{Macro} sb-fiber:with-fiber-thread (&key name) &body body
Register a main fiber on the calling thread for the dynamic extent
of @code{body} and release it on exit.  A no-op if the thread already has a
current fiber.  @code{name} is forwarded to @code{make-main-fiber}.
@end deffn
@anchor{Function sb-fiber make-main-fiber}
@ffindex @sortas{make-main-fiber sb-fiber} make-main-fiber [sb-fiber]
@deffn{Function} sb-fiber:make-main-fiber &key name current
Create a fiber representing the current thread's own stack and
register it with the thread.  If @code{current} is true, the default, the
fiber also becomes the thread's current fiber; otherwise the thread is
left with no current fiber, and @code{with-current-fiber} installs the new one
where it is needed.  @code{name} is a string label used by @code{print-object}.

Signals @code{current-fiber-error} if the thread already has a current fiber:
the code is then running on that fiber's stack, not the thread's.
@end deffn
@anchor{Macro sb-fiber with-current-fiber}
@ffindex @sortas{with-current-fiber sb-fiber} with-current-fiber [sb-fiber]
@deffn{Macro} sb-fiber:with-current-fiber (fiber) &body body
Make @code{fiber} the calling thread's current fiber for the dynamic extent
of @code{body}, and leave the thread with no current fiber on exit.  @code{fiber} must
be a main fiber of this thread, such as one made by @code{make-main-fiber} with
@code{:current} @code{nil}, and the thread must have no current fiber, so that @code{body}
runs on the stack @code{fiber} stands for; @code{current-fiber-error} is signalled
otherwise.  A no-op if @code{fiber} is already current.

The runtime's record of the running fiber is updated along with
@code{current-fiber}, which is why a library that keeps a main fiber
registered between uses should install it with this macro rather
than by writing the thread slot.
@end deffn
@anchor{Function sb-fiber current-fiber}
@ffindex @sortas{current-fiber sb-fiber} current-fiber [sb-fiber]
@deffn{Function} sb-fiber:current-fiber
Return the fiber currently running on this thread, or @code{nil} if the
thread has no main fiber.
@end deffn
@anchor{Function sb-fiber fiber-thread}
@ffindex @sortas{fiber-thread sb-fiber} fiber-thread [sb-fiber]
@deffn{Function} sb-fiber:fiber-thread instance
@end deffn
@anchor{Function sb-fiber fiber-migrate}
@ffindex @sortas{fiber-migrate sb-fiber} fiber-migrate [sb-fiber]
@deffn{Function} sb-fiber:fiber-migrate fiber dest-thread
Atomically move @code{fiber}'s ownership from its current owner thread to
@code{dest-thread}.  After this returns, @code{fiber} may be switched into only from
@code{dest-thread}.

@code{fiber} must be in state @code{:runnable}.
@end deffn
@node sb fiber lifecycle
@subsection Making and releasing fibers

A fiber made by @code{sb-fiber:make-fiber} is @code{:new} until first switched
to, @code{:runnable} while suspended, @code{:running} while it runs and
@code{:dead} once its entry function has returned. Registered fibers are
released when their thread exits; @code{sb-fiber:release-fiber} reclaims
one sooner.

@anchor{Function sb-fiber make-fiber}
@ffindex @sortas{make-fiber sb-fiber} make-fiber [sb-fiber]
@deffn{Function} sb-fiber:make-fiber function &key name stack-size binding-stack-size
Create a fiber that runs @code{function} (a zero-argument function or the
name of one) when first switched to.  @code{name} is a string label used by
@code{print-object}.

When @code{function} returns, the fiber is marked DEAD and control switches
back to its most recent resumer, delivering @code{function}'s return value
as that resumer's @code{switch-fiber} value.

The calling thread must already have a current fiber.  Use
@code{make-main-fiber} or @code{with-fiber-thread} to establish one before calling
@code{make-fiber}.  Signals an error otherwise.

Fibers are auto-released when their owning thread exits; explicit
@code{release-fiber} is only needed if you want to reclaim resources sooner.
@end deffn
@anchor{Macro sb-fiber with-fiber}
@ffindex @sortas{with-fiber sb-fiber} with-fiber [sb-fiber]
@deffn{Macro} sb-fiber:with-fiber (var function &rest make-args) &body body
Create a fiber, bind it to @code{var}, execute @code{body}, release on exit.
@end deffn
@anchor{Function sb-fiber release-fiber}
@ffindex @sortas{release-fiber sb-fiber} release-fiber [sb-fiber]
@deffn{Function} sb-fiber:release-fiber fiber
Release @code{fiber}.  For a worker fiber, unmaps its stacks and frees the
bookkeeping struct.  For a main fiber, just frees the struct; the
thread's own stack and binding stack are untouched.  Signals
@code{fiber-state-error} if @code{fiber} is a worker that is still RUNNING.
Registered fibers are released automatically when their owning thread
exits.
@end deffn
@anchor{Variable sb-fiber *default-fiber-stack-size*}
@vvindex @sortas{default-fiber-stack-size* sb-fiber} *default-fiber-stack-size* [sb-fiber]
@deffn{Variable} sb-fiber:*default-fiber-stack-size*
Default control stack size (bytes) for @code{make-fiber}.
@end deffn
@node sb fiber switching
@subsection Switching

@code{sb-fiber:switch-fiber} suspends one fiber and resumes another,
delivering values to the resumed fiber's switch, and makes the
suspended fiber the resumed one's return fiber. @code{sb-fiber:resume-fiber}
is the same with the current fiber as the one suspended, and
@code{sb-fiber:yield-fiber} switches to the current fiber's return fiber
without changing the return fiber of the target, so that a fiber
resumed from several places returns to the latest. @code{sb-fiber:join-fiber}
resumes a fiber until it finishes.

@anchor{Function sb-fiber switch-fiber}
@ffindex @sortas{switch-fiber sb-fiber} switch-fiber [sb-fiber]
@deffn{Function} sb-fiber:switch-fiber from to &rest values
Suspend @code{from} and resume @code{to}, delivering @code{values} to the resumer as
multiple values.  Sets @code{to}'s return-fiber to @code{from}, so a subsequent
@code{yield-fiber} inside @code{to} will return to @code{from}.  Returns whatever the next
switch back to @code{from} delivers, also as multiple values.
@end deffn
@anchor{Function sb-fiber resume-fiber}
@ffindex @sortas{resume-fiber sb-fiber} resume-fiber [sb-fiber]
@deffn{Function} sb-fiber:resume-fiber to &rest values
Suspend the current fiber and resume @code{to}, delivering @code{values} as
multiple values.  Equivalent to (@code{switch-fiber} (@code{current-fiber}) @code{to}
. @code{values}): sets @code{to}'s return-fiber to the current fiber, so @code{yield-fiber}
inside @code{to} comes back here.

  @code{yield-fiber}  -- suspend self, deliver to the return fiber, preserve
                  the return fiber's own caller chain.
  @code{resume-fiber} -- suspend self, deliver to a chosen fiber, make self
                  that fiber's return fiber.
  @code{switch-fiber} -- explicit FROM/TO form of @code{resume-fiber}.
@end deffn
@anchor{Function sb-fiber yield-fiber}
@ffindex @sortas{yield-fiber sb-fiber} yield-fiber [sb-fiber]
@deffn{Function} sb-fiber:yield-fiber &rest values
Switch from the current fiber to its return fiber, delivering @code{values}
as multiple values to the resumer.  Returns whatever the next
switch-in delivers (as multiple values).  Errors if the current fiber
has no recorded return fiber (never resumed).
@end deffn
@anchor{Function sb-fiber join-fiber}
@ffindex @sortas{join-fiber sb-fiber} join-fiber [sb-fiber]
@deffn{Function} sb-fiber:join-fiber fiber
Resume @code{fiber} from the current fiber until it runs to completion,
and return its entry-function values.  @code{fiber} must be RUNNABLE or NEW
and on the current thread.
@end deffn
@anchor{Function sb-fiber fiber-return-fiber}
@ffindex @sortas{fiber-return-fiber sb-fiber} fiber-return-fiber [sb-fiber]
@deffn{Function} sb-fiber:fiber-return-fiber instance
@end deffn
@anchor{Macro sb-fiber with-fiber-pinned}
@ffindex @sortas{with-fiber-pinned sb-fiber} with-fiber-pinned [sb-fiber]
@deffn{Macro} sb-fiber:with-fiber-pinned nil &body body
Increment the current fiber's pin count for the dynamic extent of
